[?25l> Now let me analyze the C code carefully and create the Rust translation. Key things to reproduce exactly:

- !! double negation (converts to 0 or 1)
- (int)double casts — undefined behavior in C for out-of-range values; Rust uses saturating casts by default, so I need as which saturates in Rust. But to match C UB, I'll need to check what the C compiler actually produces. Let me use unsafe transmute-style or as casts.
- memchr usage
- printf format strings exactly (especially %e, %ld, %d)
- (char)((seed + i * 7) % 256) — C char arithmetic with potential wrapping
- param2 % 256 etc. — C % on negative numbers gives negative results, Rust % also gives negative results for negative operands, so this matches.

For the double-to-int UB cases (INFINITY, NAN, very large negatives), C has undefined behavior. In Rust, as i32 saturates. To match C behavior on x86_64 (which uses cvttsd2si returning 0x80000000 i.e. i32::MIN for out-of-range), I need unsafe float-to-int.
